\begin_layout Title
Game Theory: Homework 6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a symmetric independent private values setting with two potential
 buyers.
 The seller owns an indivisible object which she is commonly known to value
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\in\{1,2,3\}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

 and that each realization is equally likely (occurs with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

).
 Suppose also that 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}=\{\mbox{No},1,2,3\}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

 so that there are only four possible acttions for each bidder.
 Suppose that ties are broken randomly so that each bidder acquires the
 object with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that the seller announces a second-price sealed-bid auction with
 reserve price 1.
 Find all symmetric BNE 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{*}(v)$
\end_inset

, in which the bidders play weakly dominant strategies.
 How much expected revenue is generated in this BNE?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that the seller announces a second-price sealed bid auction with
 reserve price 1.
 Find an asymmetric BNE 
\begin_inset Formula $(b_{1}(v_{1}),b_{2}(v_{2}))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that the seller announces a first-price sealed bid auction with
 reserve price of 1.
 Find the symmetric BNE, 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{**}(v)$
\end_inset

 which generates the highest expected revenue for the seller.
 How much expected revenue is generated in this BNE?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose the seller announces an all-pay auction with reserve price of 1,
 in which the bidder pays his bid independent of the auction outcome.
 Find a symmetric BNE, 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{***}(v)$
\end_inset

.
 How much expected revenue is generated in this BNE?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Consider a symmetric independent private values setting with two potential
 buyers.
 The seller owns an indivisible object which she is commonly known to value
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\in\{1,2,3\}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

 and that each realization is equally likely (occurs with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

).
 Important to note, the definition of the Bayesian Nash Equilibrium is given
 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b_{i}^{*}\left(v_{i}\right)\in\underset{b_{i}}{\textrm{argmax}}\; E\left\{ u_{i}(b_{i},b(v_{j}))\Bigr|b_{i},v_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\series bold
\bar under
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The second-price auction mechanism has the following three components: the
 set of possible bids
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{i}\in\left\{ \mbox{No},1,2,3\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

the allocation rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \textrm{if }b_{i}>b_{j}\\
\frac{1}{2} & \textrm{if }b_{i}=b_{j}\neq\mbox{No}\\
0 & \textrm{if }b_{i}<b_{j}\textrm{ or }b_{i}=\mbox{No}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and the payment rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \textrm{if }b_{i}>b_{j}=\mbox{No}\\
b_{j} & \textrm{if }b_{i}>b_{j}\neq\mbox{No}\\
\frac{1}{2}b_{i} & \textrm{if }b_{i}=b_{j}\neq\mbox{No}\\
0 & \textrm{if }b_{i}<b_{j}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the payoff for bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}=q_{i}v_{i}-p_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By Proposition 1 from Lecture 9, each bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has a weakly dominant strategy in the 
\begin_inset Formula $SPA(r,0)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b^{*}(v_{i})=\begin{cases}
\mbox{No} & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}<r=1\\
v_{i} & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}\geq r=1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

To obtain a symmetric BNE, in which the bidders play weakly dominant strategies,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in\{1,2\}:b_{i}(v_{i})=b(v_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first symmetric BNE for the second-price auction is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{*}(v_{i})=v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under this symmetric BNE, the expected payment by bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for each possible bid can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left\{ p_{i}|b_{i}=1\right\}  & = & \frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=1)=\frac{1}{6}\\
E\left\{ p_{i}|b_{i}=2\right\}  & = & P(v_{j}=1)+\frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=2)\cdot2\\
 & = & \frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{3}\cdot2=\frac{2}{3}\\
E\{p_{i}|b_{i}=3\} & = & P(v_{j}=1)+P(v_{j}=2)\cdot2+\frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=3)\cdot3\\
 & = & \frac{1}{3}+\frac{2}{3}+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{3}\cdot3=\frac{3}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the ex-ante expected payment of bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the auction is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left\{ p_{i}\right\}  & = & P(b_{i}=1)\cdot E\left\{ p_{i}|b_{i}=1\right\} +P(b_{i}=2)\cdot E\left\{ p_{i}|b_{i}=2\right\} +P(b_{i}=3)\cdot E\{p_{i}|b_{i}=3\}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{1}{6}+\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{2}{3}+\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{3}{2}=\frac{7}{9}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The expected revenue accruing to the seller is just 2 times the ex-ante
 expected payment of an individual bidder, so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left\{ \Pi_{s}\right\} =2E\left\{ p_{i}\right\} =\frac{14}{9}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, when 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=1$
\end_inset

, bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is indiffernt between bidding 1 and not bidding at all.
 So, another symmetric BNE for the second-price auction is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b(v_{i})=\begin{cases}
\mbox{No} & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=1\\
v_{i} & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}>1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Under this symmetric BNE, the expected payment by bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for each possible bid can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left\{ p_{i}|b_{i}=1\right\}  & = & 0\\
E\left\{ p_{i}|b_{i}=2\right\}  & = & P(v_{j}=1)+\frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=2)\cdot2\\
 & = & \frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{3}\cdot2=\frac{2}{3}\\
E\{p_{i}|b_{i}=3\} & = & P(v_{j}=1)+P(v_{j}=2)\cdot2+\frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=3)\cdot3\\
 & = & \frac{1}{3}+\frac{2}{3}+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{3}\cdot3=\frac{3}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the ex ante expected payment of bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the auction is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left\{ p_{i}\right\}  & = & 0+P(b_{i}=2)\cdot E\left\{ p_{i}|b_{i}=2\right\} +P(b_{i}=3)\cdot E\{p_{i}|b_{i}=3\}\\
 & = & 0+\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{2}{3}+\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{3}{2}=\frac{13}{18}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The expected revenue accruing to the seller is just the number of bidders
 in the auction times the ex-ante expected payment of an individual bidder,
 so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left\{ \Pi_{s}\right\} =2E\left\{ p_{i}\right\} =\frac{13}{9}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An asymmetric BNE 
\begin_inset Formula $(b_{1}(v_{1}),b_{2}(v_{2}))$
\end_inset

 for the second-price auction is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
b_{1}(v_{1}) & = & 3\\
b_{2}(v_{2}) & = & \mbox{No}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

If bidder 2 always bids 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

No
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, then bidder 1 has no incentive to deviate because he will always pay the
 reserve price of 1.
 If bidder 1 always bids 3, then bidder 2 has no incentive to deviate because
 he will always receive a payoff of zero.
 Hence, this is an asymmetric BNE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\series bold
\bar under
Part C
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-price auction mechanism has the following three components: the
 set of possible bids
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{i}\in\left\{ \mbox{No},1,2,3\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

the allocation rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \textrm{if }b_{i}>b_{j}\\
\frac{1}{2} & \textrm{if }b_{i}=b_{j}\neq\mbox{No}\\
0 & \textrm{if }b_{i}<b_{j}\textrm{ or }b_{i}=\mbox{No}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and the payment rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{i}=\begin{cases}
b_{i} & \textrm{if }b_{i}>b_{j}\neq\mbox{No}\\
\frac{1}{2}b_{i} & \textrm{if }b_{i}=b_{j}\neq\mbox{No}\\
0 & \textrm{if }b_{i}<b_{j}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the payoff for bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}=q_{i}v_{i}-p_{i}
\]

\end_inset

We wish to find the symmetric BNE, 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{**}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

, which generates the highest expected revenue for the seller.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b^{**}(v_{i})=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=1\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=2\\
2 & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=3
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Under this symmetric BNE, the expected payment by bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for each possible bid can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left\{ p_{i}|v_{i}=1\right\}  & = & \frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=1)+\frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=2)=\frac{1}{3}\\
E\left\{ p_{i}|v_{i}=2\right\}  & = & \frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=1)+\frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=2)=\frac{1}{3}\\
E\left\{ p_{i}|v_{i}=3\right\}  & = & P(v_{j}=1)\cdot2+P(v_{j}=2)\cdot2+\frac{1}{2}\cdot P(v_{j}=3)\cdot2\\
 & = & \frac{2}{3}+\frac{2}{3}+\frac{1}{3}=\frac{5}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the ex-ante expected payment of bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the auction is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left\{ p_{i}\right\}  & = & P(v_{i}=1)\cdot E\left\{ p_{i}|v_{i}=1\right\} +P(v_{i}=2)\cdot E\left\{ p_{i}|v_{i}=2\right\} +P(v_{i}=3)\cdot E\left\{ p_{i}|v_{i}=3\right\} \\
 & = & \frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{5}{3}=\frac{7}{9}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The expected revenue accruing to the seller is just the number of bidders
 in the auction times the ex-ante expected payment of an individual bidder,
 so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left\{ \Pi_{s}\right\} =2E\left\{ p_{i}\right\} =\frac{14}{9}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The all-pay auction mechanism has the following three components: the set
 of possible bids
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{i}\in\left\{ \mbox{No},1,2,3\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

the allocation rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \textrm{if }b_{i}>b_{j}\\
\frac{1}{2} & \textrm{if }b_{i}=b_{j}\neq\mbox{No}\\
0 & \textrm{if }b_{i}<b_{j}\textrm{ or }b_{i}=\mbox{No}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and the payment rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{i}=\begin{cases}
\mbox{0} & \mbox{if}\ b_{i}=\mbox{No}\\
b_{i} & \mbox{if}\ b_{i}\neq\mbox{No}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the payoff for bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}=q_{i}v_{i}-p_{i}
\]

\end_inset

We wish to find the symmetric BNE, 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{***}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Consider the following bidding function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b^{***}(v_{i})=\begin{cases}
\mbox{No} & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=1\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=2\\
\mbox{1} & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=3
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=1$
\end_inset

, then bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has no incentive to enter the auction because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=1)<0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=\mbox{No})=0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=2$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 is optimal because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=1)>0$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=\mbox{No})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=2)<0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=3)<0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=3$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 is optimal because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=1)=1$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=\mbox{No})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=2)=1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=3)<0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, this bidding function is a symmetric BNE since none of the bidders
 have an incentive to deviate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, the symmetric BNE which generates the highest expected revenue
 for the seller is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b^{***}(v_{i})=\begin{cases}
\mbox{No} & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=1\\
1 & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=2\\
2 & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}=3
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=1$
\end_inset

, then bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has no incentive to enter the auction because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=1)<0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=\mbox{No})=0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=2$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 is optimal because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=1)=0$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=\mbox{No})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=2)<0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|b_{i}=3)<0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=3$
\end_inset

, then 
