\begin_layout Problem
Consider a symmetric independent private values setting in which there are
 
\begin_inset Formula $n>1$
\end_inset

 buyers whose valuations are independently drawn from a uniform distribution
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Consider an all-pay auction with a reserve price and no entry fee.
 Note: in an all-pay auction, all bidders pay their bids but the bidder
 with the highest bid wins the object.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Compute the optimal reserve price for the seller 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Given 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{*}$
\end_inset

, find the symmetric BNE bidding function 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{*}(v)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate the seller's expected equilibrium revenue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is this an optimal sales mechanism from the perspective of the seller? Explain.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
The all-pay auction mechanism has the following three components: the set
 of possible bids
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{i}\in[0,\infty)
\]

\end_inset

the allocation rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \textrm{if }b_{i}>{\displaystyle \max_{j\neq i}}\ b_{j}\\
0 & \textrm{if }b_{i}<{\displaystyle \max_{j\neq i}}\ b_{j}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and the payment rule
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{i}=\begin{cases}
\mbox{0} & \mbox{if}\ b_{i}=\mbox{0}\\
b_{i} & \mbox{if}\ b_{i}\neq\mbox{0}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the payoff for bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}=q_{i}v_{i}-p_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to the Revelation Principle, given the mechanism of the all-pay
 auction 
\begin_inset Formula $(S,q,p)$
\end_inset

 and a BNE for this mechanism, there exists a direct mechanism 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{V},Q,P)$
\end_inset

 in which it is an equilibrium for each buyer to report his/her true value,
 and the outcomes are the same as in the given equilibrium of the original
 mechanism.
 Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Pi_{i}(v_{i})=Q_{i}(v_{i})v_{i}-P_{i}(v_{i})
\]

\end_inset

is the expected equilibrium payoff for bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the direct allocation mechanism where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{i}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is his expected probability of winning and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}(v_{i})=b_{i}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is his certain payment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $G(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denote the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}=\max\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{i-1},v_{i+1},\ldots v_{N}\}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
G(v_{i})=Pr(\tilde{y}_{i}<v_{i})=\prod_{j\ne i}Pr(v_{j}<v_{i})=F(v_{i})^{N-1}=v_{i}^{N-1}
\]

\end_inset

Consider the all-pay auction with reservation price 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and no entry fee, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $APA(r,0)$
\end_inset

, in which all types above some 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

 participate and use a strictly increasing bid function.
 The expected proability of winning in this all-pay auction is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q_{i}(v_{i})=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}<v_{0}\\
G(v_{i}) & \mbox{if}\ v_{i}>v_{0}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By Proposition 2 of Lecture 10, the seller's expected payoff in any BNE
 of any auction is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Pi_{s}(v_{s})=v_{s}+\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(v_{i}-\frac{1-F(v_{i})}{f(v_{i})}-v_{s}\right)Q_{i}(v_{i})f(v_{i})dv_{i}-\Pi_{i}(0)\right]
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{s}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}(0)=0$
\end_inset

, and all of the bidders are symmetric, then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Pi_{s}(0)=N{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left[v-(1-v)\right]Q_{i}(v)dv=N{\displaystyle \int_{v_{0}}^{1}}(2v-1)v^{N-1}dv
\]

\end_inset

Note that seller would like to set 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{i}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

 to maximize his expect payoff, which is equivalent to choosing the optimal
 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 This boils down to maximizing the summation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left(v_{i}-\frac{1-F(v_{i})}{f(v_{i})}-v_{s}\right)
\]

\end_inset

subject to the constraints.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}-\frac{1-F(v_{i})}{f(v_{i})}$
\end_inset

 is increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
v_{0}^{*}-\frac{1-F(v_{0}^{*})}{f(v_{0}^{*})} & = & v_{s}\\
v_{0}^{*}-(1-v_{0}^{*}) & = & 0\\
v_{0}^{*} & = & \frac{1}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, if 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=v_{0}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is indifferent between entering and exiting the auction, so 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\{u_{i}|v_{i}=v_{0}^{*}\}=\Pi_{i}(v_{0}^{*}) & = & 0\\
Q_{i}(v_{0}^{*})v_{0}^{*}-r & = & 0\\
v_{0}^{*N}-r & = & 0\\
r^{*} & = & \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In part A, we assumed that bidders follow a cut-off strategy: 0 if 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}<v_{0}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}(v_{i})$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>v_{0}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Let us suppose that all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 follow a symmetric bidding function 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v)$
\end_inset

, which is also strictly increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 and differentiable.
 Moreover, it must be true that 
\begin_inset Formula $b(0)=0$
\end_inset

.
 From part A, we know that for 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\geq v_{0}^{*}$
\end_inset

, the expected equilibrium payoff for bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi_{i}(v_{i}) & = & Q_{i}(v_{i})v_{i}-P_{i}(v_{i})\nonumber \\
\Pi_{i}(v_{i}) & = & v_{i}^{N}-b(v_{i})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Furthermore, the incentive compatibility constraint,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi_{i}(v_{i}) & = & \Pi_{i}(0)+{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{v_{i}}}Q_{i}(y)dy\nonumber \\
 & = & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{v_{0}^{*}}}0\cdot dy+{\displaystyle \int_{v_{0}^{*}}^{v_{i}}}Q_{i}(y)dy\nonumber \\
 & = & {\displaystyle \int_{\nicefrac{1}{2}}^{v_{i}}}y^{N-1}dy\nonumber \\
\Pi_{i}(v_{i}) & = & \frac{1}{N}\left[v_{i}^{N}-\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N}\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Setting equation 2 equal to equation 1 and solving for 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v_{i})$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
v_{i}^{N}-b(v_{i}) & = & \frac{1}{N}\left[v_{i}^{N}-\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N}\right]\\
b(v_{i}) & = & \left(\frac{N-1}{N}\right)v_{i}^{N}+\frac{1}{N}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, given 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{*}$
\end_inset

, the symmetric BNE bidding function 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{*}(v)$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b^{*}(v)=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\ v<\frac{1}{2}\\
\left(\frac{N-1}{N}\right)v^{N}+\frac{1}{N}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N} & \mbox{if}\ v\geq\frac{1}{2}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part C
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since bidders are symmetric, the expected revenue to the seller is 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 times the ex-ante expected payment of bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left\{ R\right\}  & = & NE\left\{ b(v)\right\} \\
 & = & N{\displaystyle \int_{\nicefrac{1}{2}}^{1}}b(v)f(v)dv\\
 & = & N{\displaystyle \int_{\nicefrac{1}{2}}^{1}}\left[\left(\frac{N-1}{N}\right)v^{N}+\frac{1}{N}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N}\right]dv\\
 & = & (N-1){\displaystyle \int_{\nicefrac{1}{2}}^{1}}v^{N}dv+\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N}{\displaystyle \int_{\nicefrac{1}{2}}^{1}}dv\\
 & = & \frac{(N-1)}{N+1}\left[1-\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N+1}\right]+\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N+1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By Proposition 3 of Lecture 10, if 
\begin_inset Formula $v-\frac{1-F(v)}{f(v)}$
\end_inset

 is monotone increasing, then the given auction is an optimal sales mechanism
 for the seller so long as the reserve price and/or entry fee is set so
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}=v_{0}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $2v-1$
\end_inset

 is monotone increasing, then the all-pay auction with 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{*}=\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{N}$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}=v_{0}^{*}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

, is an optimal sales mechanism from the perspective of the seller.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a seller(s) who wishes to sell an item to one of two potential
 buyers 
\begin_inset Formula $(i=1,2)$
\end_inset

.
 Each buyer's valuation 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is identically and independently distributed according to a uniform distributio
n with support 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 and the seller's valuation is known to be 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{s}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The seller employs an auction consisting of two stages.
 In the first stage, both buyers choose simultaneously whether to pay the
 participation fee 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 for the right to participate in the auction.
 The buyers who have paid the participation fee are then given access to
 the room in which the the auction is conducted.
 Thus, each buyer is able to observe whether the competing buyer has chosen
 to enter the auciton.
 The second stage of the auction is a simple first price auction with 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 reserve price, in which the highest bidder wins the auction and pays his
 own bid.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has observed that the competing bidder has chosen to stay out of the auction.
 Find the bid 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v_{i}|\mbox{\emph{out}})$
\end_inset

 that is part of BNE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that the bidders follow a symmetric entry strategy.
 Bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 enters the auction only if 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\geq v_{0}$
\end_inset

 and stays out if 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}<v_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Consider the second stage, in which bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 observes that both him and the competing seller have entered the auction.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{F}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denote the cumulative distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\geq v_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Find 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{F}(v)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that the bidders use a symmetric bid function 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v|\mbox{\emph{in}})$
\end_inset

 that is increasing in the bidder's valuation.
 Derive 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v|\mbox{\emph{in}})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the economic intuition behind the bidding function 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v|\mbox{\emph{in}})$
\end_inset

 that you derived in part C? Find 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

 as a function of the participation cost 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the seller's expected revenue from the auction 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{s}(c)$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the optimal participation cost 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{*}$
\end_inset

 from the point of view of the seller?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the competing bidder has chosen to stay out of the auction, then bidder
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 wins the auction with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

.
 In order to maximize his payoff, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}-c-b_{i}$
\end_inset

, bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 will bid 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v_{i}|\mbox{\emph{out}})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in\{1,2\}:v_{i}\sim U[0,1]$
\end_inset

, then the cumulative distribution function of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $Pr(v_{i}\leq v)=F(v)=v$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 We wish to find 
\begin_inset Formula $F(v|v_{i}\geq v_{0})$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
F(v|v_{i}\geq v_{0}) & = & Pr(v_{i}\leq v|v\geq v_{0})\\
 & = & \frac{Pr(v_{0}\leq v_{i}\leq v)}{Pr(v_{i}\geq v_{0})}\\
 & = & \frac{F(v)-F(v_{0})}{1-F(v_{0})}\\
\tilde{F}(v) & = & \frac{v-v_{0}}{1-v_{0}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part C
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a symmetric bidding function 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v_{i}|\mbox{\emph{in})}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
b(v_{i}|\mbox{\emph{in}}) & = & E\left[v_{j}|v_{0}<v_{j}<v_{i}\right]\\
 & = & \frac{{\displaystyle \int_{v_{0}}^{v_{i}}}v_{j}\ dv_{j}}{v_{i}-v_{0}}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{v_{i}^{2}-v_{0}^{2}}{v_{i}-v_{0}}\right)\\
b(v_{i}|\mbox{\emph{in}}) & = & \frac{1}{2}(v_{i}+v_{0})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The bidding function 
\begin_inset Formula $b(v|\mbox{\emph{in}})$
\end_inset

 that we derived in part C says that when 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 makes his bid, he does not factor in the participation cost 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 because it is now a sunk cost.
 Hence, in stage 2, bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's bid depends only on his valuation 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the cutoff value 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

 is a function of the of the participation cost 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If a bidder has valuation 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

, then he should be indifferent between participating in the auction and
 not participating.
 Such a bidder would win only if there is no competing buyer.
 So, the expected payoff of such a bidder is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Pr(v_{j}<v_{0})v_{0}-c & = & 0\\
v_{0}^{2} & = & c\\
v_{0} & = & \sqrt{c}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part E
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The equilibrium expected payment by bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
m(v_{i}) & = & Pr(v_{0}<v_{j}<v_{i})b(v_{i}|\mbox{\emph{in}})+c\\
 & = & (v_{i}-v_{0})\left[\frac{1}{2}(v_{i}+v_{0})\right]+c\\
m(v_{i}) & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(v_{i}^{2}-v_{0}^{2}\right)+c
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, the ex-ante expected payment of a bidder is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[m(v_{i})] & = & {\displaystyle \int_{v_{0}}^{1}}\frac{1}{2}\left(v_{i}^{2}-v_{0}^{2}\right)+c\\
E[m(v_{i})] & = & c(1-v_{0})-\frac{v_{0}^{2}}{2}(1-v_{0})+\frac{1}{6}(1-v_{0}^{3})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $c=v_{0}^{2}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[m(v_{i})]=\frac{c}{2}(1-\sqrt{c})+\frac{1}{6}(1-c^{\nicefrac{3}{2}})
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the expected revenue function for the seller is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\Pi_{s}(c) & = & 2E[m(v_{i})]\\
 & = & c(1-\sqrt{c})+\frac{1}{3}(1-c^{\nicefrac{3}{2}})\\
\Pi_{s}(c) & = & c-\frac{4}{3}c^{\nicefrac{3}{2}}+\frac{1}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part F
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To find the optimal participation cost 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{*}$
\end_inset

, the seller will choose 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{*}$
\end_inset

 in order to maximize 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{s}(c)$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order condition of the seller's problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1-2c^{\nicefrac{1}{2}} & = & 0\\
c^{*} & = & \frac{1}{4}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the optimal participation cost, from the seller's point of view, is
 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{*}=\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

